IMPORT prelude

-- Maritime Offences Act 2003 (Singapore) -- what is a ship and a fixed platform (s 2), the exclusion for warships and other
-- service vessels (ss 3(2), 4(5), 5(6), 6(4), 8(2)), hijacking (ss 3, 8), destroying or damaging a ship (s 4), false information
-- endangering navigation and its defences (s 5(3), (4)), threats (s 6(1)), penalties (ss 7(5), 11(4), 12(7), 13), the master's
-- power to deliver a suspect and the duties that come with it (s 12), the Public Prosecutor's consent (s 14) and extradition
-- (s 15).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/MOA2003.txt, which labels
-- itself "Informal Consolidation – version in force from 1/7/2025"; latest amendment annotated: Act 3 of 2021 wef 01/07/2025 (in
-- the s 2 definition of "act of violence"). Act 17 of 2022 (wef 01/07/2022) is annotated against s 15.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Cited by 6 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository. The whole Act is short (15 sections), so the
-- scope is most of it. Not encoded: the list of offences that make up an "act of violence" (s 2) beyond a single flag; damage to
-- navigation facilities (s 5(1), (2)); threats against navigation facilities (s 6(2)); destroying, damaging and threatening fixed
-- platforms (ss 9, 10) beyond their penalties; the deeming of acts of violence into Singapore (ss 7(1), 11(1)) beyond their
-- penalty; abetment of excluded acts (ss 7(3), 11(2)) beyond their penalty; the extradition deeming rules of s 15(5).
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Maritime Offences Act 2003 -- offences against ships and fixed platforms`

§§ `2 -- ship and fixed platform`

-- s 2: "ship" means "any vessel (including hovercraft, submersible craft and other floating craft) other than one which -- (a)
-- permanently rests on, or is permanently attached to, the seabed; or (b) has been withdrawn from navigation or laid up".
-- "fixed platform" means "any artificial island, installation or structure permanently attached to the seabed for the purpose of
-- exploration or exploitation of resources or for any other economic purpose".
DECLARE Craft HAS
    `a vessel or other floating craft` IS A BOOLEAN
    `an artificial island, installation or structure` IS A BOOLEAN
    `permanently rests on or attached to the seabed` IS A BOOLEAN
    `withdrawn from navigation or laid up` IS A BOOLEAN
    `for exploration, exploitation or another economic purpose` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the craft` IS A Craft
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the craft is a ship` IF
        `the craft`'s `a vessel or other floating craft`
    AND (NOT `the craft`'s `permanently rests on or attached to the seabed`)
    AND (NOT `the craft`'s `withdrawn from navigation or laid up`)

GIVEN `the craft` IS A Craft
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the craft is a fixed platform` IF
        `the craft`'s `an artificial island, installation or structure`
    AND `the craft`'s `permanently rests on or attached to the seabed`
    AND `the craft`'s `for exploration, exploitation or another economic purpose`

§§ `3(2), 4(5), 5(6), 6(4), 8(2) -- warships and other service vessels`

-- s 3(2) (and in the same words ss 4(5), 5(6), applied by s 6(4)): the offence "does not apply to any act committed in relation to
-- a warship, or any other ship used as a naval auxiliary or in customs or law enforcement service, unless -- (a) the person ... is
-- a citizen of Singapore; (b) the act is committed in Singapore; or (c) the ship is used in the naval, customs or law enforcement
-- service of Singapore." s 8(2) is the same for a fixed platform "used as a naval auxiliary or in customs or law enforcement
-- service" (no warship limb).
DECLARE Incident HAS
    `the target is a warship, naval auxiliary, customs or law enforcement vessel` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the target is in Singapore's naval, customs or law enforcement service` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the offender is a citizen of Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the act is committed in Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the incident` IS AN Incident
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the service-vessel exclusion applies` IF
        `the incident`'s `the target is a warship, naval auxiliary, customs or law enforcement vessel`
    AND (NOT `the incident`'s `the offender is a citizen of Singapore`)
    AND (NOT `the incident`'s `the act is committed in Singapore`)
    AND (NOT `the incident`'s `the target is in Singapore's naval, customs or law enforcement service`)

§§ `3, 8 -- hijacking`

-- s 3(1): "any person who unlawfully, by the use of force or by threats of any kind, seizes a ship or exercises control of a ship
-- shall be guilty of an offence, whatever his or her nationality or citizenship, whatever the state in which the ship is registered
-- and whether the ship is in Singapore or elsewhere." s 8(1): the same for a fixed platform. s 2: "unlawfully" means so as to be an
-- offence under any other written law in Singapore (or would be, if done in Singapore).
GIVEN `the craft` IS A Craft, `the incident` IS AN Incident, `acted unlawfully` IS A BOOLEAN, `used force or threats` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `seized or exercised control` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `hijacking is committed` IF
        (`the craft is a ship` `the craft` OR `the craft is a fixed platform` `the craft`)
    AND `acted unlawfully`
    AND `used force or threats`
    AND `seized or exercised control`
    AND (NOT `the service-vessel exclusion applies` `the incident`)

§§ `4 -- destroying or damaging a ship`

-- s 4(1): any person who "unlawfully and intentionally -- (a) destroys a ship; (b) damages a ship or its cargo so as to endanger,
-- or to be likely to endanger, the safe navigation of the ship; or (c) commits on board a ship an act of violence which is likely
-- to endanger the safe navigation of the ship". (2): "places, or causes to be placed, on a ship any device or substance which is
-- likely to destroy the ship or is likely to so damage it or its cargo as to endanger its safe navigation". (4): in Singapore or
-- elsewhere, any nationality, any flag.
DECLARE ShipHarm IS ONE OF
    `destroying the ship`
    `damaging ship or cargo so as to endanger safe navigation`
    `damaging cargo without endangering navigation`
    `violence on board likely to endanger safe navigation`
    `violence on board not likely to endanger navigation`
    `placing a device likely to destroy the ship`
    `placing a substance unlikely to cause damage`

GIVEN `the harm` IS A ShipHarm
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the harm is within section 4` IF
    CONSIDER `the harm`
    WHEN `destroying the ship` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `damaging ship or cargo so as to endanger safe navigation` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `violence on board likely to endanger safe navigation` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `placing a device likely to destroy the ship` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the craft` IS A Craft, `the incident` IS AN Incident, `acted unlawfully` IS A BOOLEAN, `acted intentionally` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the harm` IS A ShipHarm
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a section 4 offence is committed` IF
        `the craft is a ship` `the craft`
    AND `acted unlawfully`
    AND `acted intentionally`
    AND `the harm is within section 4` `the harm`
    AND (NOT `the service-vessel exclusion applies` `the incident`)

§§ `5(3), (4) -- false information endangering navigation`

-- s 5(3): "any person who intentionally communicates any information which is false in a material particular, where the
-- communication of the information endangers the safe navigation of a ship". No "unlawfully" here, and "endangers", not "likely to
-- endanger" as in s 5(1). (4): "It is a defence ... to prove that -- (a) he or she believed, and had reasonable grounds for
-- believing, that the information was true; or (b) ... he or she was lawfully employed to perform duties which consisted of or
-- included the communication of information and ... communicated the information in good faith in the performance of those
-- duties."
GIVEN `the incident` IS AN Incident, `communicated intentionally` IS A BOOLEAN, `false in a material particular` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the communication endangered safe navigation` IS A BOOLEAN, `proved reasonable belief it was true` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `proved lawful duty and good faith` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the false information offence is made out` IF
        `communicated intentionally`
    AND `false in a material particular`
    AND `the communication endangered safe navigation`
    AND (NOT `proved reasonable belief it was true`)
    AND (NOT `proved lawful duty and good faith`)
    AND (NOT `the service-vessel exclusion applies` `the incident`)

§§ `6(1) -- threats`

-- s 6(1): guilty if "(a) in order to compel any other person to do or abstain from doing any act, the person threatens that he or
-- she or some other person will do in relation to any ship an act which is an offence under section 4(1); and (b) the making of
-- that threat is likely to endanger the safe navigation of the ship." (4): s 4(5) applies.
GIVEN `the incident` IS AN Incident, `to compel another to act or abstain` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `threatened a section 4(1) act` IS A BOOLEAN, `the threat is likely to endanger safe navigation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the threat offence is committed` IF
        `to compel another to act or abstain`
    AND `threatened a section 4(1) act`
    AND `the threat is likely to endanger safe navigation`
    AND (NOT `the service-vessel exclusion applies` `the incident`)

§§ `7(5), 11(4), 12(7), 13 -- penalties`

-- s 13(1): "Any person guilty of an offence under this Act for which no penalty is expressly provided shall be liable on conviction
-- to be punished with imprisonment for life." (2): not for an act punishable under ordinary law by virtue of s 7(1) or 11(1).
-- s 7(5), 11(4): attempt "shall be punished with imprisonment for a term not exceeding 15 years". s 12(7): the master "shall be
-- liable on conviction to a fine not exceeding $5,000". s 7(3), (4), 11(2), (3): abetment -- no penalty expressly provided.
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `hijacking a ship (s 3)`
    `destroying or damaging a ship (s 4)`
    `endangering safe navigation (s 5)`
    `threatening a ship (s 6)`
    `abetting a ship offence (s 7(4))`
    `abetting an excluded act abroad from Singapore (s 7(3))`
    `attempting a ship offence (s 7(5))`
    `hijacking a fixed platform (s 8)`
    `attempting a fixed platform offence (s 11(4))`
    `violence connected with a hijacking (s 7(1))`
    `a master failing to notify or hand over evidence (s 12(7))`

DECLARE Penalty IS ONE OF
    `imprisonment for life`
    `imprisonment up to 15 years`
    `a fine up to 5000 dollars`
    `the penalty under the ordinary law for that act`

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A Penalty
DECIDE `the maximum penalty` IS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `attempting a ship offence (s 7(5))` THEN `imprisonment up to 15 years`
    WHEN `attempting a fixed platform offence (s 11(4))` THEN `imprisonment up to 15 years`
    WHEN `a master failing to notify or hand over evidence (s 12(7))` THEN `a fine up to 5000 dollars`
    WHEN `violence connected with a hijacking (s 7(1))` THEN `the penalty under the ordinary law for that act`
    OTHERWISE `imprisonment for life`

§§ `12 -- the master's power of delivery`

-- s 12(2): if the master of a ship, "wherever that ship may be, and whatever the state in which it is registered, has reasonable
-- grounds to believe that any person on board the ship has committed any relevant maritime offence in relation to any ship other
-- than a warship or other ship used as a naval auxiliary or in customs or law enforcement service, he or she may deliver that person
-- to an appropriate officer in Singapore." (3): "The master of a ship registered in Singapore may ... also deliver the person
-- concerned to an appropriate officer of any other Convention country."
DECLARE Destination IS ONE OF
    `an appropriate officer in Singapore`
    `an appropriate officer of another Convention country`
    `an officer of a country outside the Convention`

GIVEN `the destination` IS A Destination, `the ship is registered in Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `reasonable grounds to believe a relevant maritime offence` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the offence concerned a warship or service vessel` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the master may deliver the suspect` IF
        `reasonable grounds to believe a relevant maritime offence`
    AND (NOT `the offence concerned a warship or service vessel`)
    AND CONSIDER `the destination`
        WHEN `an appropriate officer in Singapore` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `an appropriate officer of another Convention country` THEN `the ship is registered in Singapore`
        OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 12(4), (5): notification of intention and reasons "must be given -- (a) before the ship in question enters the territorial
-- waters of that country; or (b) if in the circumstances it is not reasonably practicable to comply with paragraph (a), as soon as
-- reasonably practicable after the ship has entered the territorial waters of that country."
GIVEN `notified before entering territorial waters` IS A BOOLEAN, `notice before entry was reasonably practicable` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `notified as soon as reasonably practicable after entry` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the notification is in time` IF
        `notified before entering territorial waters`
    OR  ((NOT `notice before entry was reasonably practicable`) AND `notified as soon as reasonably practicable after entry`)

-- s 12(7): a master who "without reasonable excuse, contravenes subsection (4) or (6)" commits an offence. (8): a defence to a
-- subsection (4) charge "to show that -- (a) he or she believed on reasonable grounds that the giving of a notification would
-- endanger the safety of the ship; and (b) except where the country concerned is Singapore -- (i) he or she had notified some other
-- competent authority in the country concerned within the time required by subsection (5); or (ii) he or she had believed on
-- reasonable grounds that the giving of a notification to any competent authority in that country would endanger the safety of the
-- ship."
GIVEN `the destination` IS A Destination, `intends to deliver` IS A BOOLEAN, `notified in time` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN, `believed notifying would endanger the ship` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `notified another competent authority in time` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `believed notifying any authority there would endanger the ship` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the master commits the notification offence` IF
        `intends to deliver`
    AND (NOT `notified in time`)
    AND (NOT `had a reasonable excuse`)
    AND (NOT `the safety defence is shown`)
    WHERE
        `the safety defence is shown` MEANS
                `believed notifying would endanger the ship`
            AND (   `the destination` EQUALS `an appropriate officer in Singapore`
                 OR `notified another competent authority in time`
                 OR `believed notifying any authority there would endanger the ship`)

-- s 12(6): on delivering, the master "must -- (a) make ... such oral or written statements relating to the alleged offence as that
-- officer may reasonably require; and (b) deliver ... such other evidence relating to the alleged offence as is in the master's
-- possession." The s 12(8) safety defence is confined to "a contravention of subsection (4)".
GIVEN `delivered a person` IS A BOOLEAN, `gave the statements and evidence` IS A BOOLEAN, `had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `believed cooperating would endanger the ship` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the master commits the evidence offence` IF
        `delivered a person`
    AND (NOT `gave the statements and evidence`)
    AND (NOT `had a reasonable excuse`)

§§ `14 -- the Public Prosecutor's consent`

-- s 14(1): "No prosecution is to be instituted under this Act without the written consent of the Public Prosecutor." (2): even
-- without consent "(a) a person may be arrested ...; (b) a warrant for the arrest ... may be issued and executed; (c) a person may
-- be charged ...; and (d) a person charged ... may be remanded in custody or granted bail, but no further steps in the proceedings
-- ... are to be taken until the consent of the Public Prosecutor has been obtained."
DECLARE Step IS ONE OF
    `arresting the suspect`
    `issuing and executing an arrest warrant`
    `charging the suspect`
    `remanding the accused or granting bail`
    `taking a plea`
    `proceeding to trial`

GIVEN `the step` IS A Step, `the Public Prosecutor has consented in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the step may be taken` IF
        `the Public Prosecutor has consented in writing`
    OR  CONSIDER `the step`
        WHEN `taking a plea` THEN FALSE
        WHEN `proceeding to trial` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `15 -- extradition`

-- s 15(1): "Every relevant maritime offence and every relevant fixed platform offence are deemed to be extradition offences under
-- the Extradition Act 1968." (2): where no treaty is in force with a Protocol country, the Extradition Act may be applied by Gazette
-- notification "as if the only extradition offences ... were relevant maritime offences and relevant fixed platform offences".
-- (3): with a Convention country that is not a Protocol country, "as if the only extradition offences ... were relevant maritime
-- offences".
DECLARE Partner IS ONE OF
    `a Protocol country`
    `a Convention country that is not a Protocol country`

DECLARE OffenceFamily IS ONE OF
    `a relevant maritime offence`
    `a relevant fixed platform offence`

GIVEN `the partner` IS A Partner, `the family` IS AN OffenceFamily
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `extraditable under a no-treaty notification` IF
    CONSIDER `the partner`
    WHEN `a Protocol country` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a Convention country that is not a Protocol country` THEN `the family` EQUALS `a relevant maritime offence`
